{
  "teamId": 138,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 St. Louis Cardinals entered the season with playoff aspirations but quickly found themselves in an identity crisis. Opening weekend was a disaster, with closer Ryan Helsley surrendering a walk-off single to Max Muncy on March 30 and John King serving up a two-run homer the very next day. Yet the Cardinals steadied themselves in April, finishing the month at a respectable 13-13, with young shortstop Masyn Winn delivering clutch hits and Nolan Gorman flashing his power potential. It was a team finding its footing rather than one ready to contend.\n\nMay and June brought genuine optimism to Busch Stadium. Brendan Donovan emerged as a reliable offensive catalyst, delivering back-to-back high-leverage hits in late May, while a wild three-game series against the Marlins in mid-June captured the Cardinals' maddening duality — Masyn Winn launching a go-ahead homer, a remarkable double-play ending a rally, and yet the bullpen giving it all back within innings. The Cardinals went 16-12 in June, their best month of the year, peaking as a team that could grind out wins even when the moments were chaotic.\n\nJuly brought a gentle fade, and August exposed the team's fundamental flaws. The bullpen, led by a struggling Ryan Helsley, became a recurring wound — Helsley surrendered devastating doubles to Dansby Swanson and Mike Tauchman in a single August 1 game, erasing a Masyn Winn homer that had given St. Louis the lead. The team went 12-16 in August, falling out of wild card contention just as the playoff picture sharpened across the National League. Victor Scott II's clutch double and Lars Nootbaar's walk-off single offered flickers of fight, but the Cardinals could never string enough together.\n\nSeptember offered one final act of defiance — a 15-11 record that was ultimately too little, too late. The Cardinals finished 83-79, three games above .500 but well outside the postseason picture, outscored by 47 runs on the year. The season was a story of promising parts that never cohered: a young nucleus of Winn, Scott, and Gorman showing flashes, veterans like Goldschmidt and Arenado delivering moments, but a bullpen that couldn't protect leads and a run differential that told the real story. St. Louis ended the year not with a collapse but with a quiet exit — a team that was good enough to win often, but never quite good enough to matter in October.",
  "chapters": [
